<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rainer Maria Rilke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as René Karl Wilhelm Johann Josef Maria Rilke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rainer Maria Rilke was an Austrian poet, born René Rilke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, who became a significant writer in the German language and also wrote in French. He changed his first name to Rainer at the urging of Lou Andreas-Salomé, with whom he travelled twice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1912, at Duino Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, he began the Duino Elegies, which he completed in February 1922 at the Château de Muzot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he also wrote the Sonnets to Orpheus. His other works include the novel The Notebooks of Malte Laurids Brigge and the posthumous Letters to a Young Poet. He died of leukemia in December 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rainer-maria-rilke.jpg" alt="Rainer Maria Rilke" width="330" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainer_Maria_Rilke_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rilke's father, Josef Rilke, found work as a railway official after an unsuccessful military career, and his mother, Sophie Entz, came from a well-to-do <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> family. Her mourning for an infant daughter who had died within a week coloured her relationship with her only son, whom she treated as if he were a girl. His parents separated in 1884. They enrolled him in a military academy in Sankt Pölten, where he studied from 1886 until 1891 and left because of illness. After a trade school in Linz he was expelled in May 1892, returned to Prague, and passed the university entrance examination in 1895 before moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1897 Rilke met Lou Andreas-Salomé, who thought &quot;Rainer&quot; more masculine, forceful and Germanic than &quot;René&quot;. Their relationship lasted until 1900, they made two trips to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and she remained his most important confidante. He married the sculptor Clara Westhoff in 1901. In 1902 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to write a monograph on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-rodin">Auguste Rodin</a> and for a time acted as his secretary. Rodin taught him the value of objective observation, and the result was the New Poems of 1907 and 1908, noted for their thing-poems. He started his only novel, The Notebooks of Malte Laurids Brigge, in 1904 and completed it in January 1910.</p><p>Between October 1911 and May 1912 he stayed at Duino Castle, home of Princess Marie of Thurn und Taxis, where he began the Duino Elegies in 1912. They remained unfinished for a decade because of a long creativity crisis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, which surprised him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and cost him his property in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was called up in 1916 and discharged on 9 June. On 11 June 1919 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and in mid-1921 settled at the Château de Muzot, which his patron Werner Reinhart bought in May 1922 so that he could live there rent-free. In a few weeks of February 1922 he finished the Elegies and wrote the Sonnets to Orpheus, 55 sonnets in all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1923 Rilke suffered from ill health and spent long periods in a sanatorium near Montreux. His illness was diagnosed as leukemia shortly before his death. He died on 29 December 1926 in the Valmont Sanatorium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and was buried on 2 January 1927 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raron">Raron</a>. In 1929 Franz Xaver Kappus published ten letters Rilke had written to him from 1902 to 1908 as Letters to a Young Poet. Critics came to regard the Duino Elegies as Rilke's most important work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rainer Maria Rilke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
